markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>From Actions to Memory</h1>

<p>You just saw how a single click can update what's on the page. But for an app to feel truly responsive, it needs to remember information between actions, a score that goes up, a name someone typed, a search someone ran. That memory has to live somewhere. It lives in variables.</p>

<h2>From User Actions to Variables</h2>

<p>Every time a user clicks, types, or posts, the app stores and updates information behind the scenes. Programs keep track of those changes using a couple of key ideas.</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
<p>📖 <b>Variable</b> is a named container that stores a value the program can use or change.</p>
</div>

<div style="max-width:950px; margin:24px auto; display:flex; flex-wrap:nowrap; justify-content:center; align-items:stretch; gap:28px; font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; box-sizing:border-box;">

  <!-- likeCount -->
  <div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">
    <div style="background-color:#E1F3E2; border-radius:12px 12px 8px 8px; padding:12px 8px; color:#292D3E; font-size:18px; font-weight:700;">
      likeCount
    </div>

  <div style="height:145px; margin-top:3px; background-color:#FFFFFF; border:5px solid #C5EBCB; border-radius:10px; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; box-sizing:border-box; color:#292D3E; font-size:30px; font-weight:700;">
      1247
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- username -->
  <div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">
    <div style="background-color:#E3EDFC; border-radius:12px 12px 8px 8px; padding:12px 8px; color:#292D3E; font-size:18px; font-weight:700;">
      username
    </div>

  <div style="height:145px; margin-top:3px; background-color:#FFFFFF; border:5px solid #C4D9F7; border-radius:10px; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; box-sizing:border-box; color:#292D3E; font-size:30px; font-weight:700;">
      @maya
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- searchQuery -->
  <div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">
    <div style="background-color:#E9DFF8; border-radius:12px 12px 8px 8px; padding:12px 8px; color:#292D3E; font-size:18px; font-weight:700;">
      searchQuery
    </div>

  <div style="height:145px; margin-top:3px; background-color:#FFFFFF; border:5px solid #D5C3F2; border-radius:10px; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; box-sizing:border-box; color:#292D3E; font-size:30px; font-weight:700;">
      funny cats
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<p>Notice that likeCount, username, and searchQuery are just snapshots, values sitting still. Operations are what actually change them.</p>

---
## Operations change variables

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-top: 12px;">
<p>📖 <b>Operation</b> is an action that changes a variable's value.</p>
</div>


<div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/b1120de8f466532ea4fc755b2e167dad-image-1787761523343.png" alt="A diagram showing a like count of 1,247 before a click, and 1,248 after the click, illustrating an operation changing a variable's value." style="max-width: 700px; width: 100%; border-radius: 10px;">
</div>

<p>That's the whole shape of an operation: a value goes in, something changes it, a new value comes out. In code, that's exactly what you're about to see.</p>

<br>
<p>Next, you'll see exactly how a variable gets created and updated in real code.</p>


MARKDOWN
